```cpp
template <class T, size_t N>
bool operator>=(const std::array<T,N>& x, const std::array<T,N>& y) {
  for (size_t i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
    if (x[i] < y[i]) return false;
  }
  return true;
}
```

**Implementation Notes:** The seemingly simple loop above can be dramatically optimized by the compiler.  For small `N`, the compiler can unroll the loop completely, generating separate comparisons for each element.  For larger `N`, it might employ SIMD instructions (Single Instruction, Multiple Data) to compare multiple elements simultaneously.  The compiler's ability to perform these optimizations depends heavily on the underlying architecture and optimization flags used during compilation.  Furthermore, the memory layout of `std::array` – typically a contiguous block of memory – is crucial for these optimizations, enabling efficient memory access via vectorized instructions.

